\begin_layout Subsection
The scope of the thesis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This thesis covers work carried out over a period of 3.5 years of EPSRC and
 DTRA PhD funding at the School of Materials, Manchester.
 The literature review chapter discusses the motivation for this work and
 its place in the rapidly expanding field of 2D materials and their heterostruct
ures.
 The chapter will also address the challenges associated with characterising
 buried interfaces in novel structures.
 The experimental methods chapter describes the approaches used for focussed
 ion beam (FIB) sample preparation, STEM imaging of beam sensitive materials
 and extracting quantitative information from STEM image datasets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rest of the thesis focusses on three papers which use FIB sample preparation
, cross sectional STEM imaging and bespoke image processing methods to extract
 quantitative information.
 All of these papers demonstrate the unrivalled capabilites of cross sectional
 STEM analysis in these novel structures and the different image analysis
 methods available.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first paper 
\emph on
Twinning in van der Waals' materials
\emph default
 reveals novel bending phenomena in bulk crystals of graphite, hexagonal
 boron nitride and molybdenum diselenide using cross sectional STEM imaging.
 Here advanced image processing and analysis techniques allowed me to explore
 the properties of these bending phenomena and estimate their limits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second paper 
\emph on
Imaging interfaces in van der Waals’ heterostructures
\emph default
 advances the techniques applied in the first paper to characterise complex
 and beam-sensitive heterostructure systems.
 Image processing strategies employed alignment and machine learning algorythms
 to quantitatively analyse the images.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The final paper 
\emph on
Molecular transport through capillaries made with atomic-scale precision
\emph default
 relied on cross sectional STEM imaging for quality control whilst Dr Radha
 Boya developing procedures to create nanoscale capillaries within graphitic
 stacks.
 Image analysis of the cross sectional STEM images allowed the pore dimensions
 to be characterised.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Specimen fabrication for all these publications has been carried out by
 various person, all of whom are acknowledged as coauthors on the manuscript.
 Some early STEM images were taken by Dr Eric Prestat and Dr Sarah Haigh,
 and credited in place.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The thesis is summarised in the final chapter with an outlook to future
 work.
\end_layout

