\begin_layout Standard
This chapter reviews the field of van der Waals' materials, transmission
 electron microscopy (TEM) theory and methods, imaging of 2D materials and
 van der Waals' heterostructures, and optimisation of ion beam sample preparatio
n for TEM imaging.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the following sections, graphene's properties and prospects are discussed
 and the motivation for van der Waals' heterostructures outlined.
 Characterisation of 2D materials and heterostructures by different methods
 will be reviewed.
 An overview of density functional theory (DFT) calculations for van der
 Waals' heterostructures is also presented.
 DFT was carried out by coauthors of papers in chapters 4 and 5, but was
 not part of my work.
 Nevertheless, it is a powerful technique which provides useful theoretical
 comparison to the quantitative STEM data presented in the thesis, and so
 an understanding of its merits and shortcomings is useful.
 TEM methods are introduced and the motivation to use scanning transmission
 electron microscopy (STEM) presented.
 The effects of the electron beam on 2D materials is reviewed and common
 damage mechanisms discussed.
 Image and signal processing techniques used are discussed.
 Finally, FIB sample preparation for TEM imaging is reviewed, with particular
 attention paid to the challenges of this method when applied to beam sensitive
 2D materials and its limitations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Graphene, van der Waals' materials and van der Waals' heterostructures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is perhaps a hallmark of success that different communities consider
 graphene to be many different things: from a strictly free-standing single
 atomic plane of carbon, to a vast field of research encompassing all atomically
 thin crystals using van der Waals' bonding in at least one crystallographic
 axis.
 Here I will define graphene as a two-dimensional (2D) allotrope of carbon:
 a single layer of carbon atoms held together by sp
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 hybridised bonds to form a hexagonal lattice (see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Graphene_and_vdW_heterostructures"

\end_inset

)
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "novoselov2004electric"

\end_inset

.
 These sheets can be sequentially stacked together to form bilayer, trilayer
 
\emph on
etc.

\emph default
 graphene up to bulk graphite
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "geim2009graphene"

\end_inset

, wherein each graphene sheet is referred to as a basal plane
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "bernal1924thestructure"

\end_inset

.
 Van der Waals' materials can be defined as thick crystals composed of many
 layers of graphene or other 2D crystals, such as hexagonal boron nitride
 (hBN) and transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDCs).
 It should be noted that bulk graphite, and all bulk crystals with homogenous
 composition and van der Waals' bonding between atomically thin sheets,
 are included in our definition of van der Waals' materials.
 An interesting extension of this definition occurs when different 2D crystals
 are stacked upon one another to form van der Waals' heterostructures.
 We address these in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:vdW_heterostructures"

\end_inset

.
 The papers presented in chapters 4, 5 and 6 characterise both van der Waals'
 materials and van der Waals' heterostructures, and not isolated sheets
 of 2D materials.
\end_layout

