\begin_layout Standard
Graphite is a three-dimensional (3D) allotrope of carbon, and the most stable
 form of solid carbon under standard conditions (other allotropes include
 diamond, fullerenes and carbon nanotubes).
 It is the most well known van der Waals material due to its prevalent use
 as pencil lead, battery anodes and solid state lubricants.
 Graphite has a hexagonal crystal structure with lattice constants of value
 
\series bold
a 
\series default
= 2.46 Å and 
\series bold
c
\series default
 = 6.71 Å (see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:GraphiteCrystal"

\end_inset

B).
 The two planes of carbon atoms in the graphite unit cell are entirely covalentl
y bonded in-plane and these planes are held together by van der Waals' bonding.
 In crystallography these covalently bound single layers of carbon are labelled
 as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

basal planes
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 when in graphite, defined as being perpendicular to the principle axis
 of the crystal (denoted as 
\series bold
c
\series default
), and the primary plane down which the crystal can be cleaved.
 In the growing field of 2D materials, these planes are often thought of
 as graphene layers stacked on top of one another.
 The most favourable stacking configuration in graphite is that shown in
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:GraphiteCrystal"

\end_inset

, where half of the carbon atoms sit directly above the neighbouring sites
 and the other half sit in the centre of the hexagonal rings to give what
 is known as Bernal stacking.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "bernal1924thestructure,korhonen2015peeling"

\end_inset

 The distance between two basal planes is 
\series bold
c
\series default
/2 = 3.36 Å.
 This large interlayer (basal plane - basal plane) distance is a feature
 definitive of these materials.
 It arises from the balance of repulsive and attractive forces which make
 up weak van der Waals' bonding
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "berland2015vander"

\end_inset

 and allows atomically thin crystals to be cleaved and isolated from these
 materials
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "avouris2012graphene"

\end_inset

.
