\begin_layout Standard
Dry transfer is a variation on the wet transfer technique which attempts
 to minimise contamination between flakes and greatly speed up the transfer
 process
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "withers2015lightemitting,wang2013onedimensional,kretinin2014electronic"

\end_inset

.
 The wet transfer procedure is followed to the point where a flake is isolated
 on a PMMA/PCC polymer membrane above a smaller target flake.
 Once the two flakes are in contact, instead of dissolving the polymer,
 the stamp is lifted from the substrate bringing the two flakes with it,
 as shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:The-dry-transfer"

\end_inset

.
 This step is then repeated for many flakes to build up a bespoke complex
 stack of different 2D materials.
 On the last transfer step, the polymer is dissolved with acetone and the
 stack annealed.
 Through this method the majority of interfaces between flakes will be isolated
 from PMMA and solvents, greatly reducing the PMMA residue present
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "kang2012graphene,wang2013onedimensional,banszerus2015ultrahighmobility"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

