\begin_layout Subsubsection
TEM and STEM
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since TEM was first demonstrated by Ruska and Knoll in 1932
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "davidb.williams2009transmission"

\end_inset

 it has underpinned advances in materials science ever since.
 Its primary advantage comes in the form of imaging resolution, although
 the charged nature of the electrons and their different interactions with
 the specimen are what make TEM stand out as a technique.
 The wavelength of visible light is 400 - 700 nm (from blue to red) and
 Abbe's diffraction limit predicts that the highest resolution deliverable
 with conventional visible-light microscopy is roughly in the range of 300
 nm (consequently, many confocal techniques surpass this limit).
 Whilst this resolution is invaluable for imaging many structures in biology
 (e.g.
 cell structures), to look at atoms in a crystal structure we require resolution
 on the order of Ångstroms.
 This is best achieved using high energy electrons which have wavelengths
 close to 1 pm, however aberrations in the electron microscope system quickly
 become the limiting factor for resolution
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "scherzer1939ubereinige"

\end_inset

.
 Advances in lens aberration correction, and now monochromation of the electron
 beam, underpin the growing popularity and powerful application of TEM to
 a range of problems in materials science
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "batson2002subangstrom,krivanek2014vibrational"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

