\begin_layout Subsubsection
Energy dispersive X-ray Spectroscopy (EDXS) 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Analytical-EDXS"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When incident electrons inelastically scatter through an electron cloud
 (and if the accelerating voltage is high enough), core electrons of the
 atom can be kicked out to leave a hole.
 The hole is quickly filled by an electron from a higher energy orbital,
 which requires the emission of an X-ray photon.
 The photon will have the energy equal to the difference between the electrons
 initial state and when it has filled the hole.
 This photon energy is characteristic of the atom it radiates from, as the
 core orbitals are unscreened from the nucleus and so give unique photon
 energies for different elements.
 We can use this as a fingerprint to determine the composition of a specimen
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "davidb.williams2009transmission"

\end_inset

.
 Unlike many TEM techniques, EDXS requires a thick specimen to generate
 enough counts so that the signal is detectible above the background X-rays
 (named the bremsstrahlung and arising from the deceleration of electrons
 through the specimen).
 This is largely due to detector solid angles being only 0.1 – 0.3 sr, resulting
 in very poor collection efficiency of characteristic X-rays.
 Furthermore, X-rays from light elements have very low photon energies and
 are usually consigned to detection by EELS if possible (see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Analytical-EELS"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
EDXS operates differently in CTEM and STEM.
 In CTEM the entire area illuminated by the broad beam contributes to one
 spectrum, from which the composition of the specimen is inferred.
 In STEM, a spectrum can be acquired from each probe position.
 This is known as Spectrum Imaging (SI) and is very powerful as the local
 composition of nanostructures can be determined by mapping characteristic
 peak intensities across the image.
 Furthermore EDXS SI can be done in tandem with HAADF STEM imaging and BF
 imaging or EELS SI to gather multiple signals simultaneously for the same
 region of specimen.
 As X-ray emission occurs in all directions EDXS is not limited to transmission
 techniques and is also used ubiquitously in scanning electron microscope
 systems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS) 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Analytical-EELS"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS) extends the detection of the transmitte
d electrons from purely imaging to yielding extensive elemental and chemical
 information about the specimen
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "brydson2001electron"

\end_inset

.
 Incident electrons can experience a multitude of inelastic scattering events
 on passing through the specimen, resulting in them losing their initial
 energy imposed by the electron gun.
 We can seperate out these electrons by their energy loss using an electromagnet
ic prism to form a spectrum.
 The loss energies associated with different scattering phenomena are all
 present in the spectrum, and are summarised in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:EELS-spectrum"

\end_inset

.
 As we require the direct beam and weakly scattered electrons the bright
 field detector is removed from the optical axis when using the EELS detector.
\end_layout

