 Analysis of the edge intensities and fine structure gives the relative
 concentration of species in the specimen and chemical information for that
 species, respectively.
 Adapted from Egerton 2011
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "egerton2011electron"

\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:EELS-spectrum"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The low loss region (0 - 50 eV) of the spectrum is dominated by the zero
 loss peak, which contains all elastically scattered electrons from the
 specimen.
 Plasmon peaks are also seen in this region which give an indication of
 the specimen thickness.
 If the specimen is thick (e.g.
 > 90 nm for silicon) two or more plasmon peaks will be present and the
 specimen will not be suitable for EELS analysis (although EDXS count rate
 will not be problem, see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Analytical-EDXS"

\end_inset

).
 For suitably thin specimen, where only one scattering event is present,
 there will only be a single plasmon peak.
 Taking the ratio of this peak and the zero loss peak allows you to calculate
 the mean free path of an electron through the specimen.
 This can be converted into specimen thickness using the log-ratio method
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "malis1988eelslogratio,iakoubovskii2008thickness"

\end_inset

.
 It is also useful to record the low loss region for correction of peak
 shifts in the high loss region, as the energy dispersion can shift during
 acquisition.
 Additionally, the core loss EELS edges are convolved with the intense peaks
 of the loss region and should ideally be deconvolved for quantitative analysis.
 Recording both low and high loss regions is known as dual-EELS
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "davidb.williams2009transmission"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The high loss region is far less intense than the low loss, however it contains
 the most useful signals coming from the specimen.
 A core-loss edge corresponds to a scattering process wherein a core electron
 has been promoted to an unoccupied state just above the Fermi level
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "egerton2011electron"

\end_inset

.
 The position of the edge can be used to identify the element and the fine
 structure of the edge (i.e.
 small peaks after the edge onset) can provide information on the chemical
 environment of the species
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "alexander2008thereal"

\end_inset

.
 For excitations with low cross sections the signal can be very low, especially
 for higher energy excitations with a gradual edge onset.
 EELS is particularly useful for identifying light elements and is highly
 complementary to EDXS.
 
\end_layout

