\begin_layout Standard
Regardless of the specimen, the most common problem is typically contamination
 build up during imaging.
 This has been an issue in TEM for a very long time
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "egerton1976directmeasurement"

\end_inset

, however the situation has improved with advances in vacuum systems.
 Nevetheless it is still very common in all electron microscopy methods
 to experience a gradual change in image contrast with increased electron
 fluence, as mobile carbon contamination moves across the surface of the
 specimen into the highly charged area under the electron beam.
 Strategies to combat this are cooling the specimen, 'beam showering' and
 careful oxidative plasma cleaning of the specimen
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "mitchell2015contamination"

\end_inset

.
 For extremely sensitive specimen, heating by the electron beam can also
 induce changes, however these are estimated to be on the order of 1 °C
 for very thin samples in CTEM and STEM
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "egerton2004radiation"

\end_inset

.
 A far bigger problem is direct damage induced by the electron beam.
 Generally this takes the form of knock-on or radiolytic damage.
 Knock-on damage is when an incident electron collides with an atomic nucleus,
 either irreversibly displacing it from its original position or ejecting
 it from the specimen.
 The cross sections for knock-on damage for light elements, such as carbon,
 boron and nitrogen, are significant even in covalently bound systems
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "meyer2012accurate"

\end_inset

 and the electron beam energy should be reduced so that incident electrons
 do not breach the sputtering threshold of the species
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "egerton2010basicquestions"

\end_inset

.
 The knock-on cross sections for the materials used in this work are plotted
 in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Knock-on-cross-sections"

\end_inset

.
 Radiolysis is damage initiated by ionising an atom in the specimen, and
 where the hole is not filled within the timescale of phonons.
 This results in the weakened bond being torn apart by lattice vibrations,
 which in turn leads to a release of secondary electrons and generating
 a local cascade of radiolytic damage
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "egerton2013control"

\end_inset

.
 Radiolysis is mostly confined to semiconductors and insulators, as the
 dissociated electron cloud in metals almost instantaneously fills any holes
 generated.
 Radiolytic damage can be reduced somewhat by cooling the specimen to reduce
 thermal vibrations, making the specimen thinner, or increasing the electron
 beam energy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "davidb.williams2009transmission"

\end_inset

.
 The final electron beam effect we will discuss is electrostatic charging.
 A strong electrostatic field can be induced in thin, insulating specimen
 subjected to a STEM probe.
 This arises from the generation of charged species and ejection of secondary
 electrons from the specimen, and has been shown to have different polarity
 in different regions of a wedge specimen
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "jiang2016beamdamage"

\end_inset

.
 Anion and cation species are made mobile by the electrostatic field and
 can cause breakdown of the specimen structure
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "egerton2014choiceof"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some of these affects were encountered in this work due to the beam sensitive
 nature of many of the components of atomically thin van der Waals' materials,
 many of which were bulk hBN dielectric.
 Characterisation of the extent of beam damage from FIB and STEM irradition
 is demonstrated in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:FIB-induced-limits"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Advanced signal processing techniques for image and spectrum datasets
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Advanced-signal-processing"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To get quantitative information from raw TEM data some data processing is
 usually required.
 We will briefly cover some of the techniques used in this work.
 A more thorough insight into these methods is presented using experimental
 data in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Image-processing-methods"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A common technique in TEM is to convert an image to a fast Fourier transform
 (FFT), filtering or masking in the frequency domain, and using an inverse
 FFT to recover the desired data in the image.
 This is usually done with the intention of removing low frequency noise
 from the image
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "davidb.williams2009transmission"

\end_inset

 or subtracting the information from a specific lattice in order to better
 interpret the image (usually done in conjunction with image simulation)
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "meyer2011experimental"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For direct processing of images some common techniques are gaussian blur,
 for smoothing spatial fluctuations; thresholding, to generate a binary
 image to isolate specific features; edge detection, for finding the limits
 of features; Hough transforms, for detecting straight lines; and skeletonisatio
n, which reduces a multi-pixel structure down to a single thread of pixels
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "vanderwalt2014scikitimage"

\end_inset

.
 The last technique is very useful for quantitative analysis in TEM images
 as it allows the position of a lattice fringe to be expressed as an absolute
 pixel coordinate, and is used by the 'PyroMaN' software
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "dacosta2015investigating"

\end_inset

 to extract quantitative information from HRTEM lattice fringes
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "mironov2015electron,freeman2016onthe"

\end_inset

.
 Many of these processing techniques have been used in this work on HAADF
 STEM images, however each one is applied image-wise with close supervision
 of the outcome for each technique, and not as a black box algorithm applied
 to a stack of images (see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Image-processing-methods"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Multidimensional-data analysis is also possible for both STEM images and
 spectrum images
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "pena2016hyperspy"

\end_inset

.
 A dataset only requires a minimum of two dimensions: the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

signal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 axis, which plots the amplitude of some signal or measurement; and a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

navigation
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 axis, which tells us over what metric the signal fluctuates.
 More than one type of axis can be assigned e.g.
 a greyscale TEM image has two navigation axes and one signal axis, a simple
 spectrum has only one of each, and a spectrum image has three navigation
 axes (two for spatial probe position, one for energy) and one signal axis
 (X-ray or electron counts).
 By assigning axes correctly we can apply multidimensional-data analysis
 effectively to any dataset, extract the components of the signal we desire
 and map them across the navigation axes.
 A common branch of multidimensional-data analysis is blind source separation
 which attempts to disentangle a set of variables which outpopulate any
 relationships between them
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "acharyya2008anew"

\end_inset

.
 Machine learning algorithms such as principle component analysis (PCA)
 can distinguish parts of a complex signal based on their varience and,
 with some human input, be used to remove noise and identify signal components
 with physical meaning
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "hyvarinen2000independent"

\end_inset

.
 PCA has been successfully applied to multidimensional datasets in microscopy,
 such as EELS and EDXS SIs
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "delapena2011mapping,rossouw2015multicomponent,shin2016synthesis"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
(S)TEM analysis of 2D materials
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will now review TEM and STEM imaging of 2D materials, covering plan view
 (perpendicular to the basal planes) and cross sectional (down the basal
 planes) imaging.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(S)TEM imaging of 2D materials has been crucial in the rapidly expanding
 field of 2D materials as many techniques do not yield directly interpretable
 information about the atomic structure of these materials (see section
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Characterisation-of-vdWshets"

\end_inset

).
 One of the first TEM studies was to use simple diffraction methods to calibrate
 the Raman spectrum of graphene so that flake thickness and quality could
 be determined quickly and non-destructively
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "ferrari2006ramanspectrum,meyer2007thestructure"

\end_inset

.
 The properties of graphene make it an ideal specimen for TEM techniques
 as it is the thinnest possible material, is crystalline and a brilliant
 electronic conductor.
 Early imaging of pristine graphene showed the intrinsic corrugations of
 the structure
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "meyer2007thestructure"

\end_inset

, which soon led on to studying defect structures
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "meyer2008directimaging"

\end_inset

, as well as adatoms and adsorbates on the graphene surface
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "meyer2008imaging"

\end_inset

.
 Defects in graphene are particularly interesting as they can propogate
 through the lattice via simple bond rotation
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "warner2012dislocationdriven,zan2012graphene,kotakoski2014imaging"

\end_inset

 (see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Dislocations"

\end_inset

), and their concentration creates glass-like 2D amorphous structures of
 5-7 carbon rings
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "banhart2011structural,kotakoski2011frompoint"

\end_inset

.
 Analysis of dopants in graphene are of great interest: The valency of adatoms
 on graphene has been explored with EELS spectroscopy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "ramasse2013probing"

\end_inset

 and their movement through the graphene lattice tracked at atomic resolution
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "susi2014siliconcarbon"

\end_inset

.
 Graphene can also be dynamically manipulated using damage from the electron
 beam to induce morphological changes, such as sputtering to produce graphene
 nanoribbons
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "rodriguez-manzo2016insitu,wang2016fabrication"

\end_inset

 and the direct conversion of graphene to fullerene balls
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "chuvilin2010directtransformation"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

