\begin_layout Subsection
FIB preparation of lamellae for STEM imaging
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:FIB-preparation-of"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two dual-beam instruments have been used in this work: the FEI DualBeam
 Nova 600i and the Helios Nanolab DualBeam 660; both in the School of Materials,
 University of Manchester.
 Site specific preparation of cross sectional samples suitable for TEM analysis
 were prepared using the 
\emph on
in situ 
\emph default
lift-out method 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "schaffer2012samplepreparation"

\end_inset

.
 The dual-beam combines a FIB and SEM column in the same chamber and is
 also fitted with a gas-injection system to allow local material deposition
 and material-specific preferential milling to be performed by introducing
 reactive gases in the vicinity of the electron or ion probe.
 After sputtering 10 nm carbon and 3 nm Au/Pd coatings on the whole surface
 
\emph on
ex situ
\emph default
, SEM imaging of the device prior to milling allowed a suitable area to
 be identified for cross sectional TEM imaging.
 A Pt strap layer was deposited on the surface at the site of interest,
 increasing the conductive layer above the device to ~2.5 μm.
 This is shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:FIB-lamella-preparation"

\end_inset

B.
 The Pt deposition was initially done with the electron beam at 5 kV and
 1nA up to 0.5 μm in thickness.
 This is to reduce beam damage from the subsequent depostition of the ion
 beam at 30 keV Ga ions and 100pA (30keV@100pA) to build up a further 2
 μm thick deposition.
 The strap protects the region of interest during milling as well as providing
 mechanical stability to the cross sectional lamella after it is removed
 from the substrate.
 Trenches were milled around the strap using 30keV@1-6nA to produce a lamella
 of about 1 μm thick, as shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:FIB-lamella-preparation"

\end_inset

C.
 This window was all but cut free from the surface using a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

J-cut
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Before removing the bridge of material holding the lamella to the substrate,
 the top of the lamella was welded to a nanomanipulator needle using further
 Pt deposition.
 The cross sectional slice with typical dimensions of 1 μm x 5 μm x 10 μm
 could then be extracted and transferred to an Omniprobe copper half grid
 as required for TEM, shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:FIB-lamella-preparation"

\end_inset

D.
 The slice was welded onto the grid using Pt deposition so that it could
 be safely separated from the nanomanipulator by FIB milling.
 The lamella was further thinned to almost electron beam transparency using
 milling steps of 30keV@1nA, 30keV@0.23nA, 16keV@0.22nA, 8keV@66pA and 5keV@41pA.
 The final polishing step used 2keV@9pA to minimise the amorphous surface
 shell and reduce the specimen thickness to 20-70nm.
 The final lamella geometry is shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:FIB-lamella-preparation"

\end_inset

E.
 This is the most delicate step as the ion imaging has very poor resolution
 at this energy and it is very easy to thin too far (see Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Terrible_FIB_lamellae"

\end_inset

A in chapter 6).
 The fact that the cross sectional slice was precisely extracted from the
 chosen spot was confirmed for all heterostructures by comparing the positions
 of identifiable features such as Au contacts or hydrocarbon bubbles, which
 are visible both in the SEM images of the original device and within TEM
 images of the prepared cross section.
