\begin_layout Standard
The ability to bend and stretch is perhaps the most exciting property of
 2D materials (graphene, hexagonal boron nitride, molybdenum diselenide,
 etc.) and has been widely suggested as a key element for several new emerging
 technologies, such as flexible and wearable electronics, bioelectronics,
 coatings and composites[1].
 Despite a tremendous amount of current research on van der Waals crystals
 of 2D materials, little is known about what happens to their structure
 and stacking order when they are deformed.
 For virtually all crystalline materials, bending and stretching is accommodated
 through the formation of twinning defects, the geometry of which largely
 determines their mechanical behaviour.
 Twin defects are identifiable by the presence of a twin boundary; a plane
 of lattice points where the crystals on either side of the interface possess
 mirror symmetry[2].
 The formation of atomically sharp twin boundaries is ubiquitous in virtually
 all 3D materials including metals[3–5] and ceramics[6–8].
 Our understanding of twinning in graphite has stemmed from the seminal
 work on twin structures by Friese and Kelly[9], nearly six decades ago,
 and has changed remarkably little since[2,10,11] although recently there
 has been significant interest in the electronic transport properties of
 wrinkles, bends and kinks in graphene[12].
 Here we show that, yet again, 2D materials are requiring us to reimagine
 our understanding of materials’ behaviour with a completely new insight
 into the process of twinning.
 We observe that in contrast to conventional 3D materials, here twin boundaries
 have a finite width of several nm.
 Furthermore we find that uniquely for these materials the atomic structure
 of the twin defect depends crucially on the flake thickness and bend angle.
 We present the first evidence for a novel mixed mode twin structure, that
 combines discrete twin defects with local regions of nanotube-like curvature,
 and show that numerical calculations can be used to predict the different
 deformation structures we observe experimentally.
 This new knowledge of the bending behaviour of 2D materials provides vital
 foundations for tailoring transport behaviour[12], the interpretation and
 optimisation of their mechanical and electronic properties, thermal expansion
 behaviour[13]; as well as liquid- phase[14,15] and scotch-tape[16,17] exfoliati
on and crystal growth.
\end_layout

