\begin_layout Standard
Crystals of 2D materials are characterised by strong in-plane covalent bonding
 with weaker van der Waals bonding between the basal planes.
 The deformation behaviour of the crystals is therefore strongly anisotropic.
 Slip occurs readily on the basal plane[18] but is forbidden on prismatic
 planes as this would require breaking the strong in-plane bonds[2,9].
 In contrast, metallic bonding allows many crystallographic slip systems
 to be active simultaneously, leading to the ductile deformation behaviour
 of many metals.
 Twinning in metals occurs frequently during crystal growth and deformation,
 with atomic imaging of twin boundaries in metals revealing a variety of
 structures[4,5] all with the expected abrupt symmetric interface illustrated
 in material science text books[2,19].
 Twinning in polymers is known to take place by the bending of the molecules
 across the twin boundary[20] but, to our knowledge, no one has previously
 performed an atomically detailed analysis of twin boundaries in van der
 Waals crystals of 2D materials.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the latest advances in cross-sectional focussed ion beam (FIB) sample
 preparation and atomic resolution scanning transmission electron microscope
 (STEM) imaging techniques we have investigated the crystalline morphology
 of the kink band ridges that are often visible in optical microscope images
 of exfoliated flakes of 2D materials as straight line striations 1 μm wide
 (Fig.
 1a).
 We have discovered that each of the observed creases contains multiple
 twin boundaries (Fig.
 1c, d) that form as a result of strain produced by mechanical deformation
 or from differential thermal expansion during annealing on a silicon substrate.
 The atomic nature of these defects is subtle yet complex, perhaps explaining
 why they have not been formally identified despite appearing previously
 in literature TEM images[21].
 Firstly, it is clear that, when imaged at the atomic scale, these twin
 boundaries are not abrupt but diffuse over tens of atoms before the perfect
 crystal structure is recovered (Fig.
 1e,f).
 Secondly we show that the precise structure of twin defects depends on
 the crystal thickness, the bend angle, the crystallographic direction and
 the physical properties of the material.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is well known that graphene, boron nitride and many transition metal
 dichalcogenides display certain preferential low energy edges commonly
 termed armchair (ac) and zigzag (zz).
 These crystallographic directions are also the preferential directions
 for twin boundaries, and hence allow creases or kink bands to form.
 An ideal zz (ac) twin involves folding multiple basal planes about the
 zz (ac) direction as show in Fig.
 1i (1j) and characterised by an ideal twin angle .
 Here, we observe that both zz and ac direction twins in thin crystals of
 2D materials involve a gradual bending of the basal planes using incommensurate
 stacking within an extended volume of atoms across a region several nm
 wide (Fig.
 1e,f), a structure significantly different from the accepted model of twinning
 in other materials[2] (for further discussion see supplementary information
 section 1).
\end_layout

