\begin_layout Standard
The bending associated with the twin defects presented here has important
 implications for the electronic properties of the materials.
 For instance, graphene has been predicted to form p-n junctions[28] when
 bent and a similar effect in thicker films can partially account for modificati
on of the 2D Raman band[28,29].
 It is also predicted that transition metal dichalcogenides undergo significant
 change of their electronic spectrum on bending[30] which is particularly
 interesting in the context of flexible electronics applications.
 Device performance will change significantly depending on the bend defect
 type and deformation angle.
 Consequently, the defect structures demonstrated here could be employed
 to carefully study how the properties of various 2D electronic heterostructure
 devices evolve through bending.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In conclusion, we have presented the first atomic scale analysis of twin
 boundaries in van der Waals crystals of 2D materials, revealing a hitherto
 unsuspected level of complex behaviour which nevertheless can be predicted
 from numerical and first principles calculations.
 Using high resolution STEM imaging to probe the details of these fascinating
 defects, we have discovered that the deformation behaviour depends on the
 cohesive and bending properties of the material, the crystal thickness
 and bend angle.
 Thin crystals present nanotube-like bending while thicker crystals show
 discrete twins at low angles or mixed mode deformation at high angles.
 We find that a weak interface exists in mixed-mode bending that may act
 as a nucleation site for exfoliation.
 Our results provide fundamental insights for understanding the mechanisms
 of exfoliation for 2D crystal flakes, which are crucial to the development
 and application of 2D materials.
 Moreover, by revealing the thickness and angular dependence of different
 bending phenomena, we have opened new and exciting avenues in van der Waals
 heterostructure engineering for flexible electronics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thin graphite, hBN and MoSe 2 single crystal flakes were prepared on separate
 silicon substrates by mechanical exfoliation using adhesive tape.(16) For
 cross sectioning, the flakes were coated with 7 nm of amorphous carbon
 and 3 nm of Au/Pd to protect them during imaging and milling.
 A FEI Helios Nanolab DualBeam 660 instrument (incorporating a scanning
 electron microscope (SEM) and a focussed ion beam (FIB) in the same chamber)
 was used to image the flakes and produce cross sections.
 Large, uniform striations seen in almost all thick flakes were identified
 by SEM imaging and with tilting of the SEM stage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Cross sections were prepared perpendicular to the striations within the
 flakes.
 A 2 μm thick platinum layer was deposited over the region of interest (ROI)
 using a gas injection system and by patterning with the Ga
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
+
\end_layout

\end_inset

 FIB.
 Large trenches are milled either side of the ROI to a depth of 5 μm using
 high current and high energy ions to leave a window comprising (ordered
 surface to substrate) platinum, Au/Pd, carbon, crystal striation, native
 silicon oxide and the silicon substrate.
 Once the FEI EasyliftTM nanomanipulator was inserted and welded to the
 platinum layer with extra platinum, the window was cut free from the substrate
 and transferred to a pillar on a specialist OmniProbeTM TEM grid.
 The window was thinned to a TEM-transparent lamella with a thickness below
 100 nm using 30 kVe, 16 keV, 5 keV and 2 keV ion beam milling and polishing.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For high resolution scanning transmission electron microscope (STEM) imaging
 a probe side aberration corrected FEI Titan G2 80-200 kV was used with
 a probe convergence angle of 21 mrad, a HAADF inner angle of 62 mrad and
 a probe current of 80 pA.
 To ensure the electron probe was parallel to the basal planes, the cross-sectio
nal sample was aligned to the relevant Kikuchi bands of the Si substrate
 and the 2D crystal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Raman spectra of were obtained from the (0001) surfaces of the 2D crystals
 using a Renishaw inVia Raman system or Horiba LabRAM HR Evolution spectrometer,
 both equipped with HeNe lasers (λ = 633 nm).
 For most measurements a polarisation analyser was not employed.
 A 100x objective was used and the diameter of the laser spot on the crystal
 was estimated to be around 2 μm.
 The Horiba LabRAM HR Evolution spectrometer was also equipped with a second
 laser (λ = 488 nm) and this was used on some occasions for comparison purposes.
 For the polarization measurements, the polarisation of the scattered radiation
 was set to be parallel to that of the incident light using a polarisation
 analyser, and the crystal was rotated in plane to obtain the Raman spectra
 at different angles to the laser polarization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The density functional theory (DFT) calculations were performed using the
 periodic plane-wave basis set code VASP 5.35 and projector-augmented-wave
 potentials.
 The exchange–correlation functional is described by the revised Perdew–Burke–Er
nzerhof (PBE) exchange model with the empirical dispersion correction of
 Grimme (DFT-D2).
 A plane-wave cut-off of 400 eV was used for the graphene and hBN layers
 and a value of 280 eV was used for the MoSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 layers.
 We used a Monkhorst–Pack k-point grid of 11×11×1 per unit, which ensured
 that the bond lengths and energies converged to within 0.001 nm and 1 meV,
 respectively.
\end_layout

