\begin_layout Standard
The field of two dimensional (2D) crystals is expanding rapidly and now
 includes materials with a wide range of electronic properties, offering
 the potential to engineer a particular bandstructure at the atomic scale
 by layering together exfoliated crystals to build van der Waals heterostructure
s[1,2].
 This concept has facilitated the fabrication of new optical and electronic
 components[3,4] including novel transistors[5–9] and photovoltaic devices[10,11
].
 Accurate prediction of the performance of such devices relies on having
 a complete understanding of the structure including interfacial characteristics.
 However, to date there is no experimental data regarding the interfaces
 formed between dissimilar van der Waals materials and there are large uncertain
ties associated with theoretical predictions[12–14].
 Here we have measured interlayer separations for monolayer and bilayer
 transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDCs) encapsulated in hBN.
 Comparison with density functional theory (DFT) applying Becke88 van der
 Waals functionals[15] shows good agreement with MoS
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

, WS
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and NbSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 but discrepancy with experimental data for MoSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and WSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 encapsulated layers.
 We also show experimentally that the adhesion between hBN and the TMCD
 decreases on going from monolayer to bulk thickness, saturating at 3 layers.
 This trend is not reproduced by our DFT calculations and we hypothesise
 that thinner flakes are better able to deform mechanically to wet the hBN
 surface, leading to stronger adhesion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The physical and electronic properties of novel 2D multilayer architectures
 will depend critically on the nature of the buried interfaces separating
 dissimilar crystals.
 The interfacial separations are therefore key parameters when seeking to
 model the bandstructure[16,17] and adhesion strength[12] for such systems,
 and hence fundamental to understanding vertical transport data, electroluminesc
ence characteristics, interlayer diffusion and mechanical failure mechanisms[11].
 DFT is a powerful technique for predicting the ground state properties
 for encapsulated monolayers, bilayers or bulk van der Waals crystals.
 Nevertheless, challenges still exist in correctly modelling the weak dispersion
 forces between separate crystals[14].
 Current DFT approaches have been shown experimentally to work well for
 graphene and hBN heterostructure systems, however proving a DFT functional’s
 accuracy for a particular system is not trivial.
 A comparison between predicted parameters, such as interlayer separation
 and interlayer binding energy, and experimental data is required to assess
 the effectiveness of a van der Waals correction[12].
 Unfortunately such data is hard to obtain for buried interfaces in van
 der Waals heterostructures.
 In particular it is not currently available for interfaces between TMCDs
 and graphene or hBN, which are an increasingly important part of new electronic
 device structures[18].
 X-ray diffraction[19] and thermal desorption spectroscopy[20] can provide
 relevant experimental input for bulk systems but are not applicable for
 the majority of van der Waals heterostructures.
 Scanning probe techniques can yield highly accurate binding energies for
 exposed surfaces but struggles to provide information for deep interfaces.
 Cross sectional scanning transmission electron microscope (STEM) imaging
 is the only technique capable of providing atomic resolution interfacial
 data for small samples[11,21–23].
 This technique has proved invaluable in the development of traditional
 silicon based semiconductor electronics[24] and is able to provide high
 resolution structural and chemical characterisation for deep subsurface
 regions of a 2D heterostructure device[11,21–23].
 In this work we have applied lattice resolution cross sectional STEM imaging
 to study the thickness dependence on interlayer separation in van der Waals’
 heterostructures containing TMDCs.
