\series bold
1.
 HAADF STEM image processing using principle component analysis denoising
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To accurately determine the positions of the components in a van der Waals
 heterostructure the high angle annular dark field (HAADF) scanning transmission
 electron microscopy (STEM) image needs to be correctly processed.
 SI Fig.
 1A shows an atomic schematic of the structure being imaged in SI Fig.
 1C (image first aligned using hBN fringes to remove scan noise and specimen
 drift artefacts).
 To quantitatively measure the interlayer spacing between two dissimilar
 crystals (hBN – TMDC) we can take a line profile perpendicular to the lattice
 fringes, each profile with a width of one pixel (0.4 Å).
 A typical line profile taken from the raw image is shown in SI Fig.
 1D, showing that, although the peaks are visible, the exact positions of
 their maxima are not readily determined.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Principle component analysis (PCA) is a statistical algorithm which can
 be used to separate the components of a dataset by their degree of variability.[
1] A scree plot showing the explained variance ratio of each of principle
 component of the raw image is presented in SI Fig.
 1B.
 As might be expected, the components with the largest variation correspond
 to the peaks and troughs of the atomic planes in the heterostructure, whilst
 the components with the smallest variation are composed entirely of noise.
 Components between these two extremes hold some useful signal and some
 noise signal.
 As such, a judgement is made as to how many components to include in the
 reconstructed image; in this scenario we have chosen 21 components.
 SI Fig.
 1E shows the reconstructed image, which compares favourably to the original.
 The line profile in SI Fig.
 1F, however, now shows smooth peaks with readily identifiable maxima.
 This allows measurement of the distance between nearest neighbour fringes
 from these maxima with high accuracy.
 A peak finding algorithm[2], searching for local maxima was applied to
 identify the position of lattice planes.
\end_layout

