\begin_layout Standard
In this penultimate chapter we show that, just as buried interfaces between
 dissimilar crystals can be quantitatively analysed, so too can nanoscale
 porous structures.
 The paper presented here details the design, fabrication and characterisation
 of nanocapillary devices made by encapsulating arrays of lithographically
 etched graphene nanoribbons between graphite flakes.
 As is demonstrated in this paper, characterisation in addition to water
 flow measurements is nearly impossible by optical microscopy or AFM methods.
 To address this, cross sectional STEM imaging and analysis was used to
 characterise the quality of the devices and pore sizes throughout the two
 year prototyping phase.
 During this prototyping period, 18 FIB lamellae were made to characterise
 these devices.
 The devices were highly sensitive to the FIB preparation procedure, with
 early FIB lamellae proving difficult to fabricate due to delamination at
 the weak nanocapillary interface, all but destroying the lamellae.
 Some examples are shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Terrible_FIB_lamellae"

\end_inset

.
 Delamination is thought to have been induced by the electrostatic field
 generated by the ion beam during lamella preparation.
 Over the course of the two years the FIB procedure was optimised for sensitive
 specimen (see section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:FIB-preparation-of"

\end_inset

).
 Once this was achieved, shortcomings in device quality were analysed.
 The main problems were metallic inclusions left over from lithography,and
 PMMA residue which filled the nanoscale channels.
 Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Terrible_FIB_lamellae"

\end_inset

 shows some examples of these.
\end_layout

