\begin_layout Subsection
Thesis summary
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This doctorate thesis has outlined the motivation for cross sectional STEM
 imaging of van der Waals' materials and van der Waals' heterostructures,
 and the relevance of this characterisation method to this field: currently
 it is the only technique which can directly image buried interfaces and
 defects at high resolution within these complex structures.
 FIB preparation for TEM lamellae has been adapted so that the beam sensitive
 components of the structures remain undamaged and suitable for STEM analysis.
 The success of this is demonstrated by high quality HAADF imaging and analysis
 of van der Waals' materials and van der Waals' heterostructures, presented
 in publications which comprise the previous three chapters.
 Novel image processing and analysis methods were developed and applied
 to produce quantitative data describing key physical properties of defects
 and interfaces in these exotic systems.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first paper 
\emph on
Twinning in van der Waals Crystals of 2D Materials 
\emph default
investigated the nature of twin boundaries extending across multiple basal
 planes.
 Careful image processing produced isolated pixel-wide skeletons of the
 lattice fringes, from which quantitative information about the defects
 could be gathered.
 This included the slip length across the twin boundary, the radius of curvature
 of individual basal planes and the distribution of interlayer strain across
 the defect.
 By repeating this for many images for each material studied (graphite,
 hBN and MoSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

), trends between the twin boundary geometry, the bend angle and the flake
 thickness emerged and were plotted as a phase diagram.
 Finally, the source of distinct Raman signatures from twin striations in
 graphite were investigated using cross sectional STEM.
 It was found that certain slip directions cause unfavourable stacking in
 graphite, stacking faults and high lattice strain contributing to the unusual
 Raman signal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second paper 
\emph on
Imaging interfaces in van der Waals’ heterostructures
\emph default
 used cross sectional STEM imaging to measure the separation between hBN
 and many different TMDCs; interfaces common to optoelectronic devices composed
 of 2D materials
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "withers2015lightemitting,withers2015wse2lightemitting,novoselov20162dmaterials"

\end_inset

.
 It was found that all the systems studied gave good agreement between STEM
 experimental results and DFT simulations, except for monolayer MoSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and monolayer WSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 To investigate this, the interlayer distance between hBN and different
 thicknesses of WSe
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

, from monolayer to 5 layers and bulk, was determined by STEM imaging.
 A trend was revealed showing that monolayer flakes have the lowest interlayer
 distance whilst 3 layer and more have a large interlayer separation.
 As thinner flakes are better able to mechanically deform, it is thought
 that these can better wet the hBN surface and so give better adhesion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The final paper 
\emph on
Molecular transport through capillaries made with atomic-scale precision
\emph default
 required two years to develop the necessary FIB polishing procedure to
 make usable TEM lamellae.
 The main problems encountered were differential polishing of the lamella
 and delamination at weak interfaces.
 Once optimised, shortcomings in the device fabrication procedure were elucidate
d: these were mostly metallic inclusions and carbon contamination in and
 around the nanoscale capillaries.
 Again, direct imaging of these devices revealed information which is very
 difficult to obtain by any other method.
 Once clean nanocapillaries were successfully fabricated, cross sectional
 STEM imaging elucidated their structure and quantitative image analysis
 allowed the capillary height to be estimated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Future outlook
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The logical extension to this work is to improve the throughput for cross
 sectional STEM imaging.
 Currently the methods used here take a lot of FIB equipment time.
 By developing a polishing protocol using broad beam argon ion polishing,
 it may be possible to acheive even finer lamellae with reduced preparation
 time.
 In the long term, high throughput non-destructive characterisation of van
 der Waals' heterostructures will be required if they are to be realised
 commercially.
 Cross section STEM imaging will almost certainly play an important role
 in calibrating these methods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The FIB technique described in this work uses only a fraction of the instrument'
s potential.
 Recent advances in commercial instruments have lead to high resolution
 ion beams and incredibly accurate micromanipulators.
 As such ion beams have already been used to pattern some 2D materials with
 mixed results
